may not quite have clear,

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.940
so people can see the logical
flow, particularly when you're

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:33.040
blending a number of products.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.960
And I document that.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:36.280
We get it published.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:38.140
And I move on to
the next thing or I

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.280
help people to
actually understand,

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.259
if the documentation isn't
as clear as it should be,

00:09:43.259 --> 00:09:44.800
how they should be
interpreting that.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:45.560
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:46.310
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:47.230
So if people--

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:50.220
I know I've seen solutions
that have been posted on

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.330
like the
cloud.google.com website.

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:53.980
Is that your team, Miles?

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:55.090
Is that what you do?

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:56.080
MILES WARD: Yeah.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.680
100% of the material that's at
cloud.google.com/solutions is

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:01.560
stuff someone from
our team built.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.240
And a bunch of technical
documentation around

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:07.720
the business comes from
or is inspired by a lot

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:10.660
of the pathbreaking work that
we do together with customers.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:12.370
And that's exactly the model.

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:14.050
Grace is right.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:15.940
She sort of maybe
under-asserts the degree

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:19.130
to which a lot of this
comes from direct engagement

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:19.870
with customers.

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:23.410
Like Grace is working with
some of our biggest, hardest

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:24.244
opportunities.

00:10:24.244 --> 00:10:25.660
And when they get
to a place where

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:28.243
they're scratching their heads
and we're scratching our heads,

00:10:28.243 --> 00:10:30.470
we sort of dive in together
to find the answer.

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:30.970
Right?

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.680
Whether that's,
there's currently

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:35.110
no floating IP addresses
available as a part

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:36.820
of GCE networking.

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:40.090
So how do you do a software
application that depends

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:41.260
on floating IP addresses?

00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:42.670
We do the research.

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:45.190
And we dive in with customers
and do a bunch of experiments.

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:46.690
And the result is
a white paper that

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:47.830
says, here are the
four different ways you

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:50.860
can work around that problem,
that we've seen actually work.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:53.870
And that kind of
gap-filling and enablement,

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:56.150
you can sort of fake
it and just sort of,

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:58.510
ah, this is probably
how that would work.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:00.890
But when you do it
together with customers,

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:04.819
it's real and tested and
tested by somebody who really

00:11:04.819 --> 00:11:05.860
needs the answer to work.

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:09.190
So it helps us make sure
that the guidance we give

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:10.842
is something that's valuable.

00:11:10.842 --> 00:11:13.300
MELANIE WARRICK: You've talked
to a couple of the examples,

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:14.710
but can you guys talk
a little bit more

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:16.840
about the kind of customers,
and who the customers are that

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:17.770
you're working with?

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:18.478
MILES WARD: Sure.

00:11:18.478 --> 00:11:20.770
I can provide a couple
of fun examples.

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:23.410
We spent a lot of
cycles with one

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.420
of the biggest banks
in the world, HSBC,

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:26.704
in Grace's backyard.

00:11:26.704 --> 00:11:28.120
We've been working
through helping

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:32.240
them apply machine learning
and our data platform--

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.440
so tools like Dataflow
and Dataprep--

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:37.990
to be able to digest
the bank's data

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:40.600
and perform better
projections, better analysis.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.570
So one example that would be
easy is like fraud detection.

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:46.060
There's however many
millions of dollars a day

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:49.270
of fraudulent charges that
come through that bank.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:51.180
And I have to do the
guess and go, yeah,

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:53.650
I bet you every other bank
has that problem, too.

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:57.010
So we work together
with this specific bank

00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:00.250
to help them build a
fraud detection model,

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:03.940
a machine learning model, on
top of a Cloud Machine Learning

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:05.860
Engine using TensorFlow.

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:07.870
And the output of that
is higher performance

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:09.830
than the current
systems that they have.

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:11.230
So they're implementing
it into production.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.490
And we're going through and
doing the work to figure out,

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:15.281
can we turn this into
a pattern, a solution

00:12:15.281 --> 00:12:17.020
that other companies
would implement?

00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:18.954
Can we make that
pattern easy enough

00:12:18.954 --> 00:12:20.870
that our product teams
start to go, hey, maybe

00:12:20.870 --> 00:12:22.060
that should be a product.

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:25.060
It's all part of this
life cycle of discovering

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:28.630
value for customers and
helping them get pieces built.

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:30.790
Like another easy
example, you know,

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:33.100
there was a pretty
big emergency project

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:35.190
with the folks at Evernote.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:37.960
They wanted to be able to
move all of their systems

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:40.951
over to Google Cloud
in less than 90 days.

00:12:40.951 --> 00:12:41.450
Right?

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:44.980
So this is three petabytes
of customer data, hundreds

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:46.480
of independent systems.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.460
And we have a
Professional Services Team

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:51.130
that works really
hard to enable and do

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:53.120
the grunt work of
picking these pieces up.

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:55.840
But there were specific
implementation questions.

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:59.460
How do I get a shard in MySQL
to replicate over the wire?

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:01.840
How do I make it so
that we're able to tune

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:05.380
for improved performance using
Google Super Fast Solid State

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:05.920
Disk?

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:07.503
Those kinds of
questions, when there's

00:13:07.503 --> 00:13:09.040
a blocker, when
there's something

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:10.490
we don't know how to do.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:13.060
Folks from my team-- so
that one was, Brad came in,

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:15.310
Brad [? Svee ?] came in and
asked a bunch of questions

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:18.940
and was able to get down to a
best practices implementation

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:20.912
for their MySQL
system that now we're

00:13:20.912 --> 00:13:22.120
replaying to other customers.

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:24.430
So that's the model, is
find these big customers,

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:26.742
help them unpack, and
then teach the rest.

00:13:26.742 --> 00:13:29.200
MELANIE WARRICK: And how long
do these types of engagements

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:29.829
last?

00:13:29.829 --> 00:13:31.370
MILES WARD: So it's
all over the map.

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:35.050
We have a lot of conversations
where customers are really

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:37.550
just looking for that
first piece of guidance,

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:41.060
right, where maybe a little
better version of Google

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:42.800
when we Google that
for you, right?

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:45.050
Well, you know, maybe we
couldn't find the doc before,

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:46.120
but here's where it is.

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:48.850
Or you know, this is generally
this kind of best practice

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:49.870
that you want.

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:52.540
Some customers have hundreds
of problems, thousands,

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:53.860
and they can keep us busy.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:55.850
And they continue
to push platform.

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:57.600
So we have some places
where people have

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:58.850
been involved for a long time.

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:00.850
But generally, we're
looking at anywhere

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:05.170
from a superdense phone call to
a couple hours on a white board

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:07.900
to a week of kind of
a proof of concept

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:09.640
or a first implementation.

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:12.100
MARK MANDEL: So we've had
some previous podcast episodes

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.725
on talking to people
like, for example,

00:14:13.725 --> 00:14:16.090
CEs, Customer Engineers,
sort of account management

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:17.020
side of things.

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:19.862
How do you all differ
from those sort of roles?

00:14:19.862 --> 00:14:21.070
I'll pass this over to Grace.

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:22.785
Grace hasn't said
anything in a while.

00:14:22.785 --> 00:14:24.160
GRACE MOLLISON:
We don't actually

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:25.690
own any accounts, as such.

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:28.390
They tend to be owned by
the customer engineers

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:29.790
and the account managers.

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:34.600
And essentially, the
customer gatekeeper us.

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:37.680
They request our assistance,
which is how it works.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:40.810
And we also work
alongside PSO, as well.

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:42.880
So we kind of have
their backs, if you

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:45.890
like, to a certain extent,
is the way I like to see it.

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:47.590
So, as an example--

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:49.040
MARK MANDEL: And what's PSO?

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:49.956
GRACE MOLLISON: Sorry.

00:14:49.956 --> 00:14:53.500
As an example, the other day you
mentioned one of the solutions

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:55.262
that I wrote, the JCP Policies.

00:14:55.262 --> 00:14:55.970
MARK MANDEL: Yup.

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:58.840
GRACE MOLLISON: One of the CEs
of a large American company

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:02.570
saw that, passed it to their
customer, and then the customer

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:05.150
says, it'd be really good if
we could talk to the people who

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:05.780
worked on that.

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:08.320
So they got myself and the
project manager who helped

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:10.240
me work on some of that work.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.580
And we were on a call
with them yesterday

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:15.270
discussing how they should
approach things, and just

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.470
talk them through how
to use those papers.

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:18.180
So that's great.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.640
I often don't get a chance,
once it's been published,

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.830
to actually then
talk to people who

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:24.810
are right at the
beginning of even looking

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:25.770
at the documentation.

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:27.480
So that was really
a great experience.

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:28.080
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:29.820
How do people actually
reach out to you?

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:31.650
GRACE MOLLISON:
So as I said, that

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:34.950
was an example where the
CE found the blog post

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:37.500
and then sent it to the
customer and the customer

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.480
said it'd be great if
we could talk to you.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.720
It tends to be, for me
personally, in the UK,

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:45.420
tends to be people reaching out
to me, either by our Project

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:48.050
Managers, particularly
the area I focus on

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:50.040
with security and IM.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:51.810
In Europe and the
time zones, I tend

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:55.460
to be kind of a first call,
working alongside some of us

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:56.310
specialist CEs.

00:15:56.310 --> 00:15:59.800
So we have some CEs who
focus on particular areas.

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:02.570
So we've got some security
focused CEs, as well,

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:04.460
and I work alongside those.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:07.327
So there's different avenues
to get through to us.

00:16:07.327 --> 00:16:08.160
MELANIE WARRICK: OK.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.510
And are there certain
types of projects

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:11.430
that you get asked to
do that you would say

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:14.042
are not a good fit for the
solution architecture role?

00:16:14.042 --> 00:16:16.000
MILES WARD: We're no good
at making sandwiches.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:18.630
You have to say pseudo first
and then all the sudden we

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:20.740
really like making sandwiches.

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:23.340
What we don't want to do is
something we've already done.

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:25.980
Like, that's the
core is we're trying

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:30.480
to be at the front edge of
identifying how customers can

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.430
use our platform in new ways.

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:34.980
Grace can go and engage
with that customer who's

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:38.970
thinking through how to apply
policies to their business,

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:41.559
but she's going
to bring CEs with

00:16:41.559 --> 00:16:43.350
and they're going to
take a bunch of notes.

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:45.649
And hopefully, the next time
the next customer comes,

00:16:45.649 --> 00:16:47.190
they won't have to
bring Grace along.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:48.240
The CE can handle it.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:50.040
That's where Grace was
talking about with the scaling

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:50.540
function.

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:51.040
Right?

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:53.207
So, it's not that there
is any individual thing

00:16:53.207 --> 00:16:54.040
we don't want to do.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:55.650
We just don't want
to do it 10 times.

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:57.150
MARK MANDEL: So to
flip that around,

00:16:57.150 --> 00:16:58.530
it's worth pointing
out, I guess,

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:01.740
if you go to the /solutions
page on cloud.google.com,

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:04.050
these are not solutions that
were written in isolation.

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:07.920
These were written for customers
to be implemented like,

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:09.529
rubber meets the
road type stuff.

00:17:09.529 --> 00:17:09.869
GRACE MOLLISON: Yeah.

00:17:09.869 --> 00:17:10.952
MILES WARD: Yeah, exactly.

00:17:10.952 --> 00:17:12.750
Like there are so many
production customers

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:15.660
that sit on top of
those patterns that

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:17.339
are following our guidance.

00:17:17.339 --> 00:17:19.230
That's the way the role
is supposed to work.

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.105
MARK MANDEL: Do you
normally engage with say,

00:17:21.105 --> 00:17:22.650
a certain size of customer?

00:17:22.650 --> 00:17:26.200
Or is it a broad range, or
a variety of industries?

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:27.679
How does that sort of pan out?

00:17:27.679 --> 00:17:29.220
GRACE MOLLISON: I'll
take [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.220
because I happen to work a lot
on the on board and security

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:33.270
side.

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:35.700
And the complexities
you see when

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:37.500
you work with large
strategic customers,

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:40.740
I just tend to work with a
lot larger, a lot more gnarly

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:42.690
enterprisey types.

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:45.424
We've got guidance for sort
of start-ups and greenfields.

00:17:45.424 --> 00:17:47.340
But because they're
start-ups and greenfields,

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:51.600
they haven't got the existing
infra, all the issues there,

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:54.610
and everything that you need
to think about how you bring

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:57.330
yourself to the Cloud
without basically

00:17:57.330 --> 00:17:58.304
breaking your business.

00:17:58.304 --> 00:17:59.970
MILES WARD: The cool
thing with our team

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:04.760
is there's a lot of diversity
in the background and skills.

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:07.680
Cloud Platform's almost
60-something products

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:08.540
at this point.

00:18:08.540 --> 00:18:10.877
Plus, you have to sort of
know about all the hardware

00:18:10.877 --> 00:18:12.960
and software that there's
ever been on the planet.

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.930
So it's impossible
to have coverage.

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:18.930
So like if Grace spends a
bunch of cycles on identity

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:20.880
and security and kind
of enterprise setups,

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:23.430
we have folks that have a
bunch of gaming background

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:25.284
or a bunch of media
background or have

00:18:25.284 --> 00:18:27.450
spent a whole bunch of
cycles with retail companies.

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:27.949
Right?

00:18:27.949 --> 00:18:31.080
So those types of groups
have different problems.

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:34.750
And we work to resolve
issues at any size.

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:38.040
So the threshold for us is
not looking at like, you know,

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:40.050
who's the very biggest
customer possible,

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:43.410
but which is a customer that
is ready to work together

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:45.750
with us so that we can
learn as much as possible.

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:48.000
Sometimes those are little
tiny companies that are out

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:49.291
in the middle of breaking path.

00:18:49.291 --> 00:18:51.026
So, it's all over the map.

00:18:51.026 --> 00:18:53.400
MELANIE WARRICK: Miles, with
such a wide range of skills,

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:56.104
how did you find the people
that are on your team?

00:18:56.104 --> 00:18:57.270
How did you build this team?

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:04.470
MILES WARD: We're about 78%
referrals at this point.

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:09.580
So there's a phrase,
dorks can smell their own.

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:12.445
So there's a lot of us that
are old friends and that

00:19:12.445 --> 00:19:14.820
have been working on these
kinds of problems for a while.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:17.370
And we take the interviewing
and recruiting process really

00:19:17.370 --> 00:19:18.240
seriously.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:20.530
When this group gets
together, it's just

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:21.780
a bunch of buddies on a couch.

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:25.050
It's a super fun way to
connect and hang out.

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:26.580
And, you know, a
lot of peers who

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:30.360
have really seen high-scale
stuff and high-stakes stuff.

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:31.990
So we're getting
better and better

00:19:31.990 --> 00:19:34.620
every day at finding folks
that have the right attitude

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:37.020
and have the right
history of learning.

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:39.810
Even though they may be
from a new set of skills

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:41.760
that it's helping
us cover new areas,

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:43.740
that expansion is
happening all the time.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:44.760
MARK MANDEL: And are you hiring?

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:45.990
MILES WARD: Yeah, we're
absolutely hiring.

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:47.364
We've got a whole
bunch of spots.

00:19:47.364 --> 00:19:50.280
EMEA, Japan, it's a global team.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:53.190
Because here in the
US, right, if you're

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:56.040
in Mountain View doing
a startup and you

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:57.975
want to talk to an
engineer at Google,

00:19:57.975 --> 00:19:59.640
you have a lot of
different options.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:01.470
And for a single
product, often cases

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:03.840
you can get access
to, over time,

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:05.314
if you're doing
interesting stuff

00:20:05.314 --> 00:20:07.230
and pushing the boundaries,
you can get access

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.780
to engineers that are working
on the actual products.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:13.690
In Singapore, you know, we have
folks in Tel Aviv and Korea

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:17.050
and Japan, and you
know, Stockholm now,

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:18.877
and in each of those
places, it's harder

00:20:18.877 --> 00:20:20.085
to get access to an engineer.

00:20:20.085 --> 00:20:23.710
So a solutions architect also
serve as this technical point

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:26.440
of escalation inside
of the sales process

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:29.800
so that engineers in
our remote territories

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:32.660
have somebody that's at
their level to talk with.

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:35.440
A lot of that ends up
being us supporting

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:38.454
the product, management, and
product development exercise.

00:20:38.454 --> 00:20:40.870
MELANIE WARRICK: How would I
know I'm a potential solution

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:41.980
architect?

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:45.740
MILES WARD: We hire on
a Google system that--

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:48.250
they have a whole bunch of
internal labels for the stuff--

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.920
but the same kinds
of operations people

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:53.830
that get hired for
their Google SRE role

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:56.110
tend to be a good fit for us.

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:58.150
More of the
questions about Cloud

00:20:58.150 --> 00:21:00.760
have to do with how I
make this thing work

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:03.670
and keep it working
than how do I develop

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:04.810
a new application on top.

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:07.600
We have great teams
and developer relations

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:09.940
and others that are helping
our developer friends.

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:13.830
This team tends to spend
more time in operations land.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:16.630
It also tends to
look for people who

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:19.450
have a background in
hands-on operations

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:22.480
of either large-scaled,
on-premise infrastructure-- so

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:24.670
like, we have an
engineer who used

00:21:24.670 --> 00:21:27.190
to run operations for Concur.

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:28.690
And that's all
data center-based,

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:30.610
but it's the same
kinds of problems

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:33.240
they're trying to solve,
as our Cloud customers.

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:35.020
So there's a lot
of customer empathy

00:21:35.020 --> 00:21:37.310
that's possible as
a result of that.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:41.140
So another dimension
in that is Google Cloud

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:43.780
is something that you
boss around with code.

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:45.850
And it's not the
environment where,

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:48.370
you know, if somebody is just
exceptionally good at pointing

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:50.590
and clicking inside
of System Center

00:21:50.590 --> 00:21:52.610
or vCenter or
something like that,

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:54.270
that they're going
to be the right fit.

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:57.160
Our customers are asking
us to help them automate,

00:21:57.160 --> 00:21:59.530
to help them
instrument, to help them

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:01.990
turn into a dynamic
environment what has

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:04.850
been static and hard to manage.

00:22:04.850 --> 00:22:06.490
So we do have a coding bar.

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:09.280
We need people to be able
to help not just explain

00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:10.780
solutions, but develop them.

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:13.030
MARK MANDEL: So this should
segue nicely, hopefully.

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:16.090
So I'm curious, I mean, you both
work with a lot of customers.

00:22:16.090 --> 00:22:17.980
I'll direct it to you, Grace.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:19.630
Are you seeing any
particular trends

00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:22.060
in terms of the help that
customers are needing,

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:23.870
or the sort of things
that they're doing?

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:24.744
GRACE MOLLISON: Yeah.

00:22:24.744 --> 00:22:27.610
So as I said,
that's how I've been

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:29.860
able to produce some
of the work I've done.

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:32.020
I actually work very
closely with customers.

00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:34.340
And you start seeing a pattern.

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:35.890
Once you start seeing
those patterns,

00:22:35.890 --> 00:22:37.306
you can actually
then convert them

00:22:37.306 --> 00:22:40.300
into guides and documents
and tutorials and reference

00:22:40.300 --> 00:22:41.690
architectures.

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:44.290
So as an example,
every customer wants

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:46.960
to understand how
they can actually

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:51.170
map their job, their
functional roles, to IM roles.

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:53.380
That's something that
you just need to learn

00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:54.610
how to do at the beginning.

00:22:54.610 --> 00:22:56.350
So you know, I
just say to people,

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:57.730
step away from the keyboard.

00:22:57.730 --> 00:22:59.350
Think about your planning.

00:22:59.350 --> 00:23:01.450
Map those role to your IM roles.

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:03.630
And then you can
start implementing it.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:05.110
Folks also want
to understand how

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:06.680
to automate things, as well.

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:09.610
So that's a very common
question is like,

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:11.410
are they hybrid multi-Cloud?

00:23:11.410 --> 00:23:14.560
Do they want to use OSS
solution like Terraform?

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:16.590
Or do they, if
they're all into GCP,

00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:18.700
then maybe they have
used Deployment Manager.

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:20.800
So it helps them make
those sorts of decisions,

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:23.564
you know, and looking at the
skill sets they've got already

00:23:23.564 --> 00:23:25.480
and just bringing them
on the journey, really.

00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:26.320
MELANIE WARRICK: Grace,
what do you enjoy

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:27.784
most about the work you do?

00:23:27.784 --> 00:23:29.950
GRACE MOLLISON: I love the
fact that every week I've

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:31.450
learned something new.

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:35.950
I also love the fact that I'm
asked to work with customers.

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:38.740
You know, I have a lot
of respect for our CEs,

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:41.340
particularly, because
they've got a hard job.

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:43.310
And I love working with them.

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:44.650
They are very collaborative.

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:48.354
And you know, I get up every
day really enthused about work.

00:23:48.354 --> 00:23:50.020
MELANIE WARRICK: I
know when we both met

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:52.515
before this, I had
asked you, would there

00:23:52.515 --> 00:23:54.890
be anything else you would do
other than being a solution

00:23:54.890 --> 00:23:55.969
architect.

00:23:55.969 --> 00:23:57.510
GRACE MOLLISON: And
I said then, it's

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:01.060
really, really hard to think
of another role that fits me,

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:04.370
personally, and the
stuff that I like to do.

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:08.500
And I think if I
was put on the spot,

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:10.920
maybe I'd look at doing
something into DevRel,

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:13.686
or maybe working in the PM Org.

00:24:13.686 --> 00:24:15.560
I mean, they're the two
roles that would fit.

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:19.500
Because I used to do
a CE type role at AWS,

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:21.160
so I know how hard that is.

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:21.820
I've done that.

00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:23.330
And it's like evolving.

00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:25.660
And at the moment, I
really enjoy this job.

00:24:25.660 --> 00:24:27.550
And I can't see
anything that rivals it.

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:29.500
MELANIE WARRICK: You like
everything but Miles, right?

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:29.710
GRACE MOLLISON: Oh, no.

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:30.340
He's terrible.

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:36.640
MILES WARD: There's
this phrase that there

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:38.900
are kind of two kinds
of people in the world,

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:42.100
folks that are entropy
creators, and folks

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:44.140
that are entropy destroyers.

00:24:44.140 --> 00:24:46.480
I think you can sort of
make a quick guess as

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:50.507
to which side of that dividing
line Grace and I each land on.

00:24:50.507 --> 00:24:52.840
MARK MANDEL: I know you've
published a lot of solutions,

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:54.730
and maybe you can't
necessarily name names,

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:56.740
but what's been the most
interesting solution

00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:58.491
that you've had to
provide for a customer?

00:24:58.491 --> 00:24:59.948
Grace, we were just
talking to you.

00:24:59.948 --> 00:25:00.930
Why don't you go ahead?

00:25:00.930 --> 00:25:03.190
GRACE MOLLISON: So,
ironically enough, it's

00:25:03.190 --> 00:25:05.750
pretty much in the first
six months that I joined.

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:09.490
I was helping work
with the IM team,

00:25:09.490 --> 00:25:11.834
regarding helping
them get it to GA.

00:25:11.834 --> 00:25:13.500
We had no real knowledge
at that moment.

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:14.870
How was this going to work?

00:25:14.870 --> 00:25:17.060
How would customers go
to interface with it?

00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:20.040
So that was a lot of talking
to a lot of big customers

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:21.540
who were able to
give us some input.

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:24.130
So people like
Spotify, for example,

00:25:24.130 --> 00:25:25.760
provided a lot of
the input there.

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:27.050
And then it evolved.

00:25:27.050 --> 00:25:29.450
So the latest set of
docs, I would say,

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:33.500
were the ones I actually enjoyed
the most because it took some

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:36.140
of the early work and
then it was blending it

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:37.560
with lots of different things.

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:40.940
So I looked at billing
and I looked at networking

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:43.700
and I looked at, you know,
the Cloud Resource Manager.

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:46.260
And I had all of
these bits and I've

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:48.710
got to actually figure
out a logical path

00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:51.020
to make it clear and
easy for our customers.

00:25:51.020 --> 00:25:52.520
You know, so it was
at the beginning

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:55.220
and the most recent stuff
were the two things I really

00:25:55.220 --> 00:25:56.962
enjoyed working on, I think.

00:25:56.962 --> 00:25:59.170
MELANIE WARRICK: So, I've
done consulting in my past.

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.461
And I know one of the biggest
challenges we were always

00:26:01.461 --> 00:26:04.010
up against was scope
creep, and trying to keep,

00:26:04.010 --> 00:26:08.270
especially customers, within
the bounds of expectations

00:26:08.270 --> 00:26:09.770
and realities of time.

00:26:09.770 --> 00:26:11.480
I assume that's one
of your challenges

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:13.940
that you're up against,
in terms of this role.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:15.710
How do you usually
work with that?

00:26:15.710 --> 00:26:18.620
MILES WARD: I'll say that
because the definition

00:26:18.620 --> 00:26:24.209
of the team is relatively clear,
that when a customer is blocked

00:26:24.209 --> 00:26:25.250
either at the beginning--

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:26.958
I don't think this is
possible on Cloud--

00:26:26.958 --> 00:26:29.290
or at the end-- hey, you
people said this was possible

00:26:29.290 --> 00:26:31.070
on Cloud--

00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:32.727
when they get
blocked and we don't

00:26:32.727 --> 00:26:34.310
know how to solve
that problem, that's

00:26:34.310 --> 00:26:36.530
exactly when solutions
architects get involved.

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:39.290
And similarly, when
they are unblocked

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:40.880
and they appear to
be able to continue

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:42.890
to move without our
support and assistance,

00:26:42.890 --> 00:26:44.340
that's when we go away.

00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:46.520
So far, the scope
creep has not been

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:49.100
from the individual projects.

00:26:49.100 --> 00:26:52.130
Like generally, people ask
for relatively specific things

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:54.700
because that's the current
long pole in the tent

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:56.000
that they need resolved.

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.509
But there's certainly a
very small number of us

00:26:58.509 --> 00:27:00.050
and a very large
number of customers.

00:27:00.050 --> 00:27:02.660
And so I think the actual
creep is more about,

00:27:02.660 --> 00:27:05.630
can we put you at every
customer everywhere?

00:27:05.630 --> 00:27:07.055
And that's relatively
complicated

00:27:07.055 --> 00:27:08.930
because there's hundreds
of thousands of them

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:09.971
and there's dozens of us.

00:27:09.971 --> 00:27:12.302
So the creep is, the
creep is internal.

00:27:12.302 --> 00:27:14.510
MELANIE WARRICK: That sounds
like a constant juggling

00:27:14.510 --> 00:27:15.720
of prioritizing.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:17.360
And what are some
other challenges

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:19.340
that you are working
with in terms

00:27:19.340 --> 00:27:21.860
of managing this team, Miles?

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:25.440
MILES WARD: My big
job is an easy one.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:29.990
It's helping our folks continue
to learn the new hard bits.

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:31.620
It's really easy to--

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:33.620
and everybody does this--
you find a place where

00:27:33.620 --> 00:27:35.200
you have expertise.

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:38.060
You get relied on for
that expertise by others.

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:40.970
But the world of technology
changes so rapidly

00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:44.990
that any amount of real
experience and depth in a given

00:27:44.990 --> 00:27:47.790
area has value for
about a decade.

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:49.945
So we have some of
our folks on our team

00:27:49.945 --> 00:27:51.570
that have been here
for half that long.

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:53.194
So all of a sudden,
you're going, well,

00:27:53.194 --> 00:27:54.860
you're halfway to
having the experience

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:57.440
that you had at the
beginning be not worthwhile.

00:27:57.440 --> 00:27:59.340
Maybe you should
learn some new stuff.

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:02.000
So there's a whole
bunch of training

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:03.860
and, you know, some
of that is autodidact.

00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:05.390
People dive into
whole new pieces.

00:28:05.390 --> 00:28:07.910
Some of it is stuff that
we help people through.

00:28:07.910 --> 00:28:11.600
In a lot of cases, following
the solutions architect model,

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:13.090
that's training
that we go build.

00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:14.690
That's the best we
know how to learn.

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:20.090
So we didn't have GCP 101,
like the most basic onboarding

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:22.550
training materials to teach
people how this stuff worked.

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:25.520
And Grace had just
started so she wrote it.

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:28.030
And now we have a
training class, right?

00:28:28.030 --> 00:28:29.780
Like that was her
way of figuring out

00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:31.180
how to run all this stuff.

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:32.960
And that's why we like her.

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:33.920
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, and
then off of the learning

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:35.600
point, Grace, what
are some things

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:37.980
that you're excited to
learn about in the future,

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:39.000
in the near future?

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:40.880
GRACE MOLLISON: So I'm
actually pivoting now

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:44.750
to spend more time with the
data loss prevention, the API.

00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:47.430
And I'm working very
closely with the PM there.

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:50.000
So I'm really enjoying that
because it's a really fun

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:52.070
product and it's so underrated.

00:28:52.070 --> 00:28:53.540
It's just very, very powerful.

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:57.320
And obviously with all the
things regarding PII and making

00:28:57.320 --> 00:29:00.350
sure that you can stop sensitive
data getting out early,

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:01.700
it's just a key tool.

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:02.270
That's fine.

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:04.350
And I'm also going to be doing
some work with the stack driver

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:05.190
team, as well.

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:09.080
So it's still in the same area,
but there's just new things

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:09.622
all the time.

00:29:09.622 --> 00:29:10.371
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:29:10.371 --> 00:29:11.790
So we're running
out of time here.

00:29:11.790 --> 00:29:14.510
But before we wrap up, is there
anything that either of you

00:29:14.510 --> 00:29:18.200
want to plug or mention or talk
about before we finish things

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:18.720
up here?

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:19.390
Miles?

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:23.010
MILES WARD: We're in the
middle of building some new--

00:29:23.010 --> 00:29:25.730
and I'm pretty excited
about-- some new things

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:30.140
to do at our big events, NEXT
and the Global Summit Series.

00:29:30.140 --> 00:29:32.147
Those are great
opportunities to connect

00:29:32.147 --> 00:29:33.230
with solutions architects.

00:29:33.230 --> 00:29:35.150
SAs are always there.

00:29:35.150 --> 00:29:38.834
And it's a great way to get
maybe the 15 minute version

00:29:38.834 --> 00:29:40.250
of an engagement,
where we can get

00:29:40.250 --> 00:29:43.369
on our big whiteboard that's got
all of the architecture icons

00:29:43.369 --> 00:29:45.410
and systems and you can
come and hang out with us

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:48.130
and we'll help figure out how
to solve the problem that you've

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:49.250
got.

