00:31:05.705 --> 00:31:07.420
Let me ask you the
Question of the Week.

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:10.370
How do I get it a Docker
image into Minikube

00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:12.820
without uploading it
to an external registry

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:15.110
and then downloading
it all over again?

00:31:15.110 --> 00:31:17.110
Is there an easier way
to do this locally?

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:17.860
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:31:17.860 --> 00:31:20.350
So this is near and dear to
my heart, being Kubernetes.

00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:22.300
So Minikube is a
really nice tool

00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:24.190
for running a Kubernetes
cluster locally.

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:26.020
So usually, what
it does is it'll

00:31:26.020 --> 00:31:29.070
run a small virtual machine
that has Kubernetes installed

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:30.330
as a single node installation.

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:31.288
And that's really nice.

00:31:31.288 --> 00:31:33.070
The interesting thing
is when you're like,

00:31:33.070 --> 00:31:34.450
oh, I built my image locally.

00:31:34.450 --> 00:31:37.090
How do I get it into the VM
so that Kubernetes cluster

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:37.720
can see it?

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:39.560
There's a couple of
interesting ways you can do it,

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:41.710
one of which is you can
actually build inside Minikube.

00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:42.740
There's ways of doing that.

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:44.740
You can actually mount
your code inside the VM.

00:31:44.740 --> 00:31:45.239
That's good.

00:31:45.239 --> 00:31:45.874
That works.

00:31:45.874 --> 00:31:47.290
But it means you
might necessarily

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:48.490
be double building stuff.

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:50.323
There's other options
where I've seen people

00:31:50.323 --> 00:31:53.717
who have installed their own
Docker registry inside Minikube

00:31:53.717 --> 00:31:56.050
so that they can push to that
and then sort of pull down

00:31:56.050 --> 00:31:56.900
locally from there.

00:31:56.900 --> 00:31:59.180
But there's an even easier
way, which I really like,

00:31:59.180 --> 00:32:00.721
which works if you're
running a Linux

00:32:00.721 --> 00:32:03.660
terminal of any kind,
which is you can actually

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:07.769
pipe the Docker image into
Minikube, which is really cool.

00:32:07.769 --> 00:32:09.310
So if you use
Minikube-- and I'll put

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:11.800
a link to the exact command--
but if you've used Minikube,

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:13.320
if you say Minikube
Docker environment,

00:32:13.320 --> 00:32:14.650
it will set the
Docker environments

00:32:14.650 --> 00:32:16.859
up so that Docker knows,
oh, I'm not running locally.

00:32:16.859 --> 00:32:18.400
I'm going to push
all my stuff there.

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:20.760
And there are two commands,
Docker Save and Docker Load.

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:24.340
So Docker Save will take a
image and put it into a file

00:32:24.340 --> 00:32:26.380
or put it into a
like output stream.

00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:29.290
And Docker Load can take a
file and import it or take it

00:32:29.290 --> 00:32:30.302
from the input stream.

00:32:30.302 --> 00:32:31.510
So we basically just pipe it.

00:32:31.510 --> 00:32:33.620
So you do a Docker Save
with the image you want,

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:36.484
pipe it into any [? value ?]
of the Docker environment.

00:32:36.484 --> 00:32:38.650
And then Docker Load and
then it pushes it right in.

00:32:38.650 --> 00:32:40.024
You don't need to
local registry.

00:32:40.024 --> 00:32:41.100
You can build locally.

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:42.230
Actually, it's really nice.

00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:42.850
I like it a lot.

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:43.766
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

